<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="xylogalacturonan biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5980" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;About Pectin&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Pectin &quot;Pectin&quot;| is one of the major components of the primary cell wall of plants. It is composed of four structurally distinct polysaccharides, namely |FRAME: 1-4-alpha-D-galacturonosyl &quot;homogalacturonan&quot;| (HG), rhamnogalcturonan I (RG-I), rhamnogalacturonan II (RG-II) and |FRAME: Xylogalacturonan &quot;xylogalacturonan&quot;| (XGA). Although all four components appear to be present in all cells, their relative abundance and structural details differ among cell types and species |CITS: [17827695]|. Pectin is synthesized in Golgi vesicles. The structural complexity of pectin suggests that a large number of enzymes is involved in the process, including glycosyltransferases, methyltransferases, acetyltransferases and feruloyltransferases. Evidence indicates a role for pectin in plant growth, development, morphogenesis, defense, cell adhesion, wall structure, signaling, cell expansion, wall porosity, binding of ions, growth factors and enzymes, pollen tube growth, seed hydration, leaf abscission, and fruit development |CITS: [15377216][11554482]|. Pectin is also used in the food and cosmetic industries, and has been shown to have several positive effects on human health |CITS: [17513886]|. &lt;b&gt;About Xylogalacturonan&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Xylogalacturonan &quot;Xylogalacturonan&quot;| is a substituted |FRAME: 1-4-alpha-D-galacturonosyl &quot;homogalacturonan&quot;| in which the &amp;alpha;-1,4-linked |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE| backbone is substituted at position O-3 with a &amp;beta; linked |FRAME: CPD-25028|. Occasionally the xylose is further substituted at position O-4 with an additional &amp;beta; linked xylose. The homogalacturonan backbone is partially methylesterified at the C-6 carboxyl, and may be O-acetylated at positions O-2 or O-3. |FRAME: Xylogalacturonan &quot;Xylogalacturonan&quot;| has been reported to be abundant in the reproductive tissues of many plants. Recently it was reported to be present in vegetative tissues of Arabidopsis |CITS: [17336350]|.</Description>
